question:
The output of the following C Program is:
#include <stdio.h>
int main (void)
{
    int x, *p;
    x=30;
    p=x;
    print f (“%d", *p);
    return 0;
}

solution:
Here p=x; is given.
In this line, p is a pointer to an integer (int *), and
x is an integer. However, the assignment p = x;
attempts to assign an integer (x) to a pointer (p).
This will result in a compilation error because we
cannot assign an integer directly to a pointer.
Corrected program should be as follows-
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void)
{
    int x, *p;
    x = 30;
    p = &x;
    printf("%d", *p);
    return 0;
}
Explanation of Corrected Program:
int x, *p;: Declares an integer x and a pointer
to an integer p.
x = 30;: Assigns the value 30 to the integer x.
p = &x;: Assigns the address of x to the pointer
p.
printf("%d", *p);: Prints the value of x (which is
30) by dereferencing p.
return 0;: Indicates successful completion of
main().
This corrected version of the program will
output 30, which is the value stored in x.
So option D is the correct answer.

